WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.323
Picture a renewal notice. It is clear, accurate and identical

00:00:03.323 --> 00:00:06.646
for everyone. One person reads it and calls with a

00:00:06.646 --> 00:00:09.969
question. Another reads it as a final denial and gives

00:00:09.969 --> 00:00:13.292
up. A third never opens it, because past letters from

00:00:13.292 --> 00:00:16.615
government brought bad news. The notice did not change. The

00:00:16.615 --> 00:00:17.280
reader did.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.563
Culture shapes how people read what an agency sends, how

00:00:20.563 --> 00:00:23.497
they disagree in a meeting and whether they trust the

00:00:23.497 --> 00:00:24.670
person on the phone.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:28.365
Most leaders accept this. The harder question is how to

00:00:28.365 --> 00:00:31.711
get better at working with it. Here the research offers

00:00:31.711 --> 00:00:32.380
three surprises.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:36.111
First, people are poor judges of their own skill. In

00:00:36.111 --> 00:00:39.491
one nursing school, 98 percent of students rated their intercultural

00:00:39.491 --> 00:00:42.872
competence higher than an assessment did. A broad review of

00:00:42.872 --> 00:00:46.253
self-assessment found that people have only moderate insight into their

00:00:46.253 --> 00:00:49.633
abilities. Insight is weakest for complex skills, and this is

00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:53.014
a complex skill. Feeling comfortable is not the same as

00:00:53.014 --> 00:00:53.690
being effective.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.640
Second, exposure is not enough. A study followed nearly thirteen

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.240
hundred college students. Those who studied abroad gained only about

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.840
two points on an intercultural measure. Students who took classes

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.440
mainly alongside local students, the most immersive group, barely changed

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.040
at all. Students who met with a cultural mentor gained

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.640
more. Contact with difference, without help to make sense of

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:19.240
it, can confirm old assumptions as easily as challenge them.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:23.361
Third, one-time training fades. A review of 260 training studies

00:01:23.361 --> 00:01:27.133
found that knowledge held up over time, but changes in

00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:30.904
attitude faded. Training worked better when it built skills, ran

00:01:30.904 --> 00:01:34.676
over a longer period and connected to other changes in

00:01:34.676 --> 00:01:35.430
the organization.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.767
What about the models and assessments you may have encountered?

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:41.755
They are useful maps. They remind you that people differ

00:01:41.755 --> 00:01:44.743
in how they make sense of difference, not just in

00:01:44.743 --> 00:01:47.730
what they know. But a score is one questionnaire on

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.718
one day. No study found here shows it predicts how

00:01:50.718 --> 00:01:53.705
someone does their job. Never use a score to label

00:01:53.705 --> 00:01:54.900
yourself or anyone else.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:58.581
So what works? Practice with guidance, on real work, again

00:01:58.581 --> 00:02:01.911
and again. After a hard call or a tense meeting,

00:02:01.911 --> 00:02:05.242
take ten minutes with a colleague who was there. Ask

00:02:05.242 --> 00:02:08.572
three questions. What did I assume? What might the other

00:02:08.572 --> 00:02:11.570
person have experienced? What will I try next time?

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.848
Then look at the decisions you control: the channel, the

00:02:14.848 --> 00:02:17.775
deadline, the meeting time, the script. Ask who each one

00:02:17.775 --> 00:02:20.703
works for, and who has to do extra work to

00:02:20.703 --> 00:02:23.631
use it. That is where culture stops being an idea

00:02:23.631 --> 00:02:25.680
and becomes part of how you lead.
